## Notes for RTEMS 6.1 ARM64 port of ION 4.1.2 with BPv7

This port is a **minimal BP/LTP implementation** targeting RTEMS 6.1 on 64-bit ARM (AArch64) platforms. It represents a significant modernization of the previous RTEMS 5.1 port with the following key changes:

### Major Changes from RTEMS 5.1 Port

* **RTEMS 6.1**: Upgraded from RTEMS 5.1 with API compatibility updates
* **64-bit ARM (AArch64)**: Migrated from 32-bit SPARC (erc32) to 64-bit ARM (a53_lp64_qemu)
* **ION Admin Public API**: Uses modern programmatic configuration (no config files required)
* **SPACE_ORDER=3**: Updated for 64-bit pointer addressing
* **Minimal Feature Set**: BP + LTP only (excludes AMS, BSS, CFDP, NM)
* **Memory Configuration**: Increased heap and workspace sizes for 64-bit

### Excluded Components

* **CFDP** (CCSDS File Delivery Protocol) - excluded via `NASA_PROTECTED_FLIGHT_CODE`
* **AMS/RAMS** (Asynchronous Message Service)
* **BSS** (Bundle Streaming Service) - Note: BSSP is included as BP dependency
* **NM** (Network Management)

### Transport Layer

* **LTP over POSIX Message Queues**: Uses `pmqlso`/`pmqlsi` for loopback testing
* **No libbsd required**: Pure RTEMS POSIX implementation
* **Future enhancement**: UDP support can be added with RTEMS libbsd

### Prerequisites

1. Install RTEMS 6.1 toolchain for AArch64
2. Build RTEMS 6.1 BSP for `aarch64/a53_lp64_qemu`
3. Set PATH to include RTEMS 6.1 tools

Example RTEMS 6.1 setup:
```bash
export PATH=$HOME/rtems/6/bin:"$PATH"
```

Refer to [RTEMS 6.1 documentation](https://docs.rtems.org/branches/master/) for detailed installation instructions.

### Building the Port

1. Navigate to the `arch-rtems` directory
2. Create source symlinks: `./srclinks`
3. Configure with WAF:
   ```bash
   ./waf configure --rtems=$HOME/rtems/6 --rtems-bsp=aarch64/a53_lp64_qemu
   ```
4. Build:
   ```bash
   ./waf
   ```
5. The output executable is `build/ion.exe`

### Configuration Details

**Memory Configuration (64-bit ARM):**
* `wmSize`: 200,000 bytes (increased from 100,000)
* `heapWords`: 150,000 words (increased from 75,000)
* `sdrWmSize`: 200,000 bytes (increased from 100,000)
* RTEMS workspace automatically sized

**ION Configuration:**
* Node Number: 19
* Loopback only (127.0.0.1)
* LTP span: node 19 to node 19 via POSIX message queues
* IPN scheme with endpoints: ipn:19.0, ipn:19.1, ipn:19.2, ipn:19.64, ipn:19.65, ipn:19.126, ipn:19.127

**RTEMS Resource Limits:**
* 40 POSIX threads
* 100 POSIX semaphores
* 10 POSIX message queues
* 40 file descriptors
* 40 tasks
* 20 semaphores

### Testing

The port includes a built-in loopback test that:
1. Initializes ION with the public API
2. Starts all BP/LTP services
3. Runs `bpsource` and `bpsink` for a simple bundle transfer
4. Cleanly shuts down all services

Run in QEMU:
```bash
qemu-system-aarch64 -M xilinx-zynq-a9 -m 256M -no-reboot -serial null \
  -serial mon:stdio -nographic -kernel build/ion.exe
```

Expected output:
```
=== ION RTEMS 6.1 ARM64 Port - Minimal BP/LTP ===
Starting ION with public API (no config files)...
Initializing ION...
Starting RFX clock...
Initializing LTP...
Initializing BP...
Starting BP...
ION startup complete.
Starting loopback test.
Loopback test ended.
Stopping ION...
```

### Architecture Notes

**Public API vs Config Files:**

The RTEMS 5.1 port used configuration files (`.ionrc`, `.ltprc`, `.bprc`) which required:
* File I/O operations
* Text parsing
* POSIX shell execution (`pseudoshell`)

The RTEMS 6.1 port uses the ION Admin Public API which provides:
* Direct C function calls (`ionInitialize`, `add_span`, `add_endpoint`, etc.)
* No filesystem dependencies
* Better error handling
* Easier integration with RTEMS applications
* More suitable for embedded systems

**Build System:**

Uses WAF (Waf build system) with rtems_waf integration:
* Builds static libraries: `libici.a`, `libltp.a`, `libdgr.a`, `libbssp.a`, `libbp.a`
* Links into single RTEMS executable: `ion.exe`
* DGR library included as BP dependency
* Symbol table (`mysymtab.c`) for dynamic task spawning

### Known Limitations

* Loopback only (no real network interfaces)
* No CFDP file transfer capability
* No AMS multicast support
* Limited to single-node operation with POSIX message queues
* UDP/TCP convergence layers not included (can be added with libbsd)

### Future Enhancements

* Add UDP support with RTEMS libbsd
* Enable CFDP (resolve build issues)
* Port to real ARM hardware (Raspberry Pi 4, Xilinx ZynqMP, etc.)
* Add TCP convergence layers
* Multi-node testing over real networks

---

## Archive: Notes for RTEMS 5.1 port of ION 4.1.2

This section preserved for historical reference. See above for current RTEMS 6.1 ARM64 port.

This port followed the basic features scope and approach of previous ION 3.6 port with the following changes:

* Updates to RTEMS 5.1 release
* Updates port from BPv6 to BPv7
* Updates RTEMS build system from `make` to `waf`, builds individual libraries
* Target: sparc/erc32 (32-bit system)
* Does not include CFDP and NM modules

---









## Archive: Notes for RTEMS 4.10.2 port of ION 3.6

The RTEMS port of ION in this directory is just a demo template from which
real RTEMS ION-based systems can be cloned and adapted.  To develop an RTEMS
ION-based system modeled on this template you will want to modify the RTEMS
linkcmds file to reflect the actual memory resources provided, modify
ionrtems.c considerably (or develop an entirely new Init module that does
the same general things; in particular, upgrade the RTEMS configuration
definitions as needed), develop new software that uses ION for communications,
modify the Makefile accordingly (possibly adding to the list of MANAGERS), etc.

A step-by-step procedure for demo'ing ION on RTEMS follows.

First construct the RTEMS development environment:

1.	Use the Yum Instructions at

	http://www.rtems.com/wiki/index.php/APT/Yum_Repository

to download and install the pre-built RTEMS development tools.  Install the
appropriate rpm, then use yum install to install the tools.

This ION RTEMS port was done on a 32-bit Fedora 20 host system using rtems
branch 4.10.2, selecting the SPARC "sis" simulator as the target system; this
is similar to the approach taken in the Quick Start example at

	http://www.rtems.com/wiki/index.php/Quick_Start

For the balance of this README, SPARC "sis" for RTEMS 4.10.2 will be assumed;
substitute other version, system, or bsp as appropriate.

2.	Make development directories:

	$(HOME)/rtems
	$(HOME)/rtems/archive
	$(HOME)/rtems/tools
	$(HOME)/rtems/tools/build

3.	Use the links in the Getting Started manual from the appropriate online
documentation library (http://www.rtems.com/onlinedocs/releases) to download
the RTEMS source code and the examples into $(HOME)/rtems/archive.

4.	Continue following the instructions in the on-line manual for
installing the operating system.  This entails adding

	export PATH=/opt/rtems-4.10/bin:${PATH}

to ~/.bash_profile before doing anything.

Test the tools as explained in the online manual.

Then, in rtems/tools/build, enter the following commands to build RTEMS:

	../rtems-4.10.2/configure --target=sparc-rtems4.10 --enable-rtemsbsp=sis --prefix=/opt/rtems-4.10/
	make all install

5.	Test the RTEMS development environment.  First add

	export RTEMS_MAKEFILE_PATH=/opt/rtems-4.10/sparc-rtems4.10/sis

to ~/.bash_profile (and be sure (!) to source ~/.bash_profile in any window in
which you plan to build any RTEMS application).

Then unpack the sample applications and build hello_world_c described in
the online manual.  The compiled application can be run from the Linux
command line by entering

	sparc-rtems4.10-run `find . -name hello.exe`

Alternatively, to run and debug the application within gdb, enter

	sparc-rtems4.10-gdb `find . -name hello.exe`

and enter the following commands at the gdb prompt:

	tar sim
	load
	r

6.	Now build and run the ION RTEMS demo application.  First cd into
ion/arch-rtems and enter the following commands to populate the directory
with symbolic links to the ION C source files and build the ion.exe executable:

	./srclinks
	make

Then use the following command to run the demo.

	sparc-rtems4.10-run `find . -name ion.exe`

The ION daemons will start running and a test execution of bpsource will
send a loopback bundle via LTP to bpsink, which will print the text in the
bundle payload.

The ION RTEMS Makefile is configured to optimize the resulting executable.
To see the actual size of the executable (with the debugging information 
inserted by the compiler removed):

$ cd o-optimize
$ cp ion.exe stripped.ion.exe
$ sparc-rtems4.10-strip stripped.ion.exe
$ ls -l stripped.ion.exe
$ sparc-rtems4.10-size stripped.ion.exe

Optimization can make source-level debugging more difficult, because key
variables may be held in registers rather than in memory.  To disable
optimization, remove the "-O2" parameter from the CFLAGS variable in the
Makefile and rebuild.
